0.50.293.090 D Parsed message: {"role":"assistant","content":"The color of the sky depends on how far sunlight has to travel through the atmosphere to reach your eyes, and how the air particles interact with that light.\n\nSunlight looks white, but it is actually made of all the colors of the rainbow. When this light hits the Earth's atmosphere, it bumps into gas molecules and tiny particles. This causes the light to scatter in different directions. Shorter wavelengths (blue and violet) scatter much more easily than longer wavelengths (red and orange).\n\n**At noon**, the sun is directly overhead. The sunlight travels a relatively short distance through the atmosphere to get to you. Because the path is short, the blue light scatters in all directions, filling the entire sky with a bright blue glow. Your eyes are also more sensitive to blue than violet, which is why we see blue instead of violet.\n\n**At sunset**, the sun is low on the horizon. The sunlight must travel through a much thicker layer of the atmosphere to reach your eyes. By the time the light gets through, most of the blue light has already scattered away long before it reaches you. What remains are the longer wavelengths—reds, oranges, and yellows—which travel straight through the air with less scattering, painting the sky in warm colors.\n\n**"}
