1.14.023.869 D Parsed message: {"role":"assistant","content":"Making sourdough bread is a journey of patience and biology, relying on a symbiotic culture of wild yeast and lactobacilli (bacteria) known as the **starter**. Unlike commercial yeast, which is added directly, sourdough requires feeding and maintaining this living culture before it can leaven the dough.\n\nHere is the step-by-step process from starter maintenance to the final loaf, followed by an analysis of why loaves often turn out dense.\n\n### The Process: From Starter to Loaf\n\n#### 1. Feeding and Maintaining the Starter\nBefore baking, you must ensure your starter is active and healthy.\n*   **Feeding:** You discard a portion of the existing starter (usually half) and replace it with equal parts flour and water by weight. This refreshes the food supply for the microbes.\n*   **Peak Activity:** After feeding, the starter needs time to ferment. It will rise, bubble, and smell tangy. The ideal time to bake is when the starter has doubled in volume and shows signs of \"hunger\" (starting to collapse slightly) just before it peaks. This ensures maximum gas production during baking.\n\n#### 2. Mixing and Autolyse\n*   **Mixing:** Combine"}
